The table is ordered by population. The four coastal districts — the Guledga Capital District, Webhose, Barwaqo and Geeso — front the [[Capuyaquiran Sea]] along the southwestern shore. The eastern districts border [[Asikyira]], whose territory includes the former [[Libertalia]]; the western and northern districts border [[Tapuya]], and the far north adjoins [[Potocsí]].

== Names ==

The names of the southern districts were given by Burawi settlers in the sixteenth and seventeenth centuries. [[Guledga]] is named for [[Guled Badweyn]], the founder of the colony. [[Webhose]] combines ''webi'' ("river") and ''hose'' ("lower"), and names the country of the lower river. [[Barwaqo]] is the Burawi word for abundance. [[Geeso]], from ''gees'' ("corner"), names the southwestern corner of the country. [[Dhuldhaxe]] combines ''dhul'' ("land") and ''dhaxe'' ("middle"), the country between the coast and the mountains. [[Dahabga]] combines ''dahab'' ("gold") with the place suffix ''-ga'', and names the gold-bearing country annexed in the [[Lacashe-Asikyira War]].

The names of the northern districts are Burawi renderings of toponyms in the {{W|Tapuya language}}, the speech of the [[Tapuya Confederacy]] that occupied the interior lowlands before the colonial conquest. The Tapuya population was reduced to a small remnant by the late eighteenth century, and the language survives in Lacashe principally in these place names.

