The renderings follow regular adaptations of Tapuya sounds to Burawi orthography: Tapuya ''p'' becomes Burawi ''b'', ''ü'' becomes ''u'', and ''tr'' becomes ''d''. Several names combine a Tapuya root with a Burawi suffix, the place suffixes ''-ga'' and ''-o''. The same toponymy appears in the dual name of the [[Matarrañya / Cañadapirén]], the mountain range that forms the interior boundary with Asikyira.

== History ==

The civil divisions of the colonial [[Province of Lacashe]] were administered from [[Guledga]] through magistracies, with the parishes of the [[Burawi Catholic Church]] providing the register of settlement. The districts of the independent republic, constituted after 1789, continued the boundaries of the colonial magistracies.

The [[Lacashe-Asikyira War]] of 1838–1840 added approximately 5,000 square kilometres of gold-bearing mountain territory along the eastern border. The annexed territory lies within the present [[Dahabga]] District, whose name records the deposits.

The [[Treaty of Guledga]] of 1856 transferred the eastern coastal strip to the new [[Libertalia|Free Republic of Libertalia]]. The districts of that strip ceased to form part of Lacashe. Libertalia united with [[Asikyira]] in 1889, and the former Libertalian territory now constitutes the eastern border of the Webhose and Dhuldhaxe districts.

The present boundaries were drawn under the constitution of 1983, which retained the district as the unit of local administration and separated the capital into the [[Guledga Capital District]].

== Government ==

Each district is administered by a district council and a district administrator. Below the districts, administration is carried out by municipalities and communes. The [[National Assembly of Lacashe]] is elected in part through single-member constituencies drawn within the districts.

